  {
    "episode_id": 75272,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75272.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "report that was filed. It says that the committee recognizes that the standard occupational classification system or all of you know it as the SOC, categorization of a public safety telecommunicator as an office of administrative support occupation. It recognizes that it's outdated and does not reflect the nature of this life-saving work. And the committee notes that OMB has not follow the instructions of the committee to reclassified public safety telecommunicators under the SLC and to correctly classify them as protective service occupation. So, in essence, it says it directs OMB to reexamine the classification by 2023, but it also has another caveat that it says, and to correctly classify them as protective service. occupation. We went as far as we could in directing the office to do their job. It's really unfortunate that we have not been able to get a committee hearing on the SAVES Act. The best approach to ensuring that this actually happens is to put more teeth into the law. And that is to have legislation introduced it. We have introduced it in the 116th Congress. We introduced it again in the 117th Congress. It was co-authored by our principal co-author, it was Republican Brian Fitzpatrick of Pennsylvania and that was in the 117th Congress. It was also co-sponsored by 130 members of Congress. 94 of those were Democrats and 36 of those were Republicans. So this is a bipartisan effort that we are trying to accomplish here. We recognize the work of these heroes, unsung heroes, by the way, that are heard and never seen. So in this fiscal year, 2023, what we did was we took part of the language from that bill, and we included it in the must pass omnibus, which is the budget bill that for funding the federal government.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
