  {
    "episode_id": 75272,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75272.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "It is critically important. We just have to, you know, turn on the news, the mass shootings that are, you know, chronic in our communities, not just that, but, you know, crime in general. 911 dispatchers have been stretched, you know, to to all different directions. They have, you know, their job duties have evolved as technology continues to evolve. Ten years from now, you know, their job could look very, very different. But the law has stayed the same. And the law has failed to recognize that they need professional training. They need better skills to cope with the internal PTSD from listening, you know, call after call after call to very traumatic incidents that are occurring in our communities. but also to be better at handling those calls, at alleviating some of the stress that our callers are under when they're dialing 911, at asking those really hard to get answers, those questions that must be asked in order for our first responders, whether they're police officers or paramedics, to get them the information that they need, whether there's weapons involved, or what kind of you know, medical emergency. The person is under so that they can provide life threatening or life saving assistance when they get there. They're prepared with the equipment. That takes a lot of training. And I think that we have failed to recognize and we have left it to local jurisdictions to deal with the cost of the attrition rate among these professionals. We know the attrition rate is horrendous because they, you know, three years on the job, they are already burned out. They don't have the mental health support that they need. in order to continue to do this job. So, you know, we can't leave it to local governments whose budgets are already stressed. And every time, you know, the police department budgets are reduced, the 911 dispatchers are the first to be cut. Those positions are frozen. They don't hire new people. They don't replace retiring dispatchers. So that's why I think the focus needs to continue in this profession. I want to thank you and your listeners and all of those 911 dispatchers that continue to share the 911 stories from their perspective. All of those tragic incidents that they deal with day to day That's important. When I'm asking now is if you work at a PSAP, you know, call our office, give us some information as to, you know, who we can coordinate with in your PSAP when we have information. to give you whether it's grants that are coming up that are being created because part of the omnibus bill, it also includes funding for training for these workers. So we want to make sure that every PSAP has the same opportunity to train their employees.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
