  {
    "episode_id": 75272,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75272.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Yes, absolutely. And I think that we have to look at this technology and how it helps and hinders the work of the 911 dispatcher. Text to 911 is wonderful. It's just another way to communicate your call for assistance. or the crime that is that you are watching, right? But it robs that dispatcher with the ability to be able to hear what is happening in the room. So for a burglary in progress, when somebody is actually breaking in into your home. You know, somebody calling 911, the dispatcher is able to hear glass break is able to hear voices is able to hear footsteps. The caller doesn't have to say anything. You know, that dispatcher is the witness to that crime. With video to 911, it's going to be much more traumatic for those dispatchers because they will be able to see real life crime unfolding, not just listen to it, but they will be able to see it. And for those reasons, we need to ensure that every person answering, working in this profession has access to mental health. They have to be able to have closure when they're talking with victims through these circumstances.",
