  {
    "episode_id": 75272,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75272.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "I think about 911 dispatchers pretty much every single day, you know, whether I'm at home or I'm working, I'm thinking about them. Holidays, I'm thinking about them because, you know, it's 24 seven, they're always open and people call them during their worst time in their lives. And I think about myself in the victim's shoes, you know, January 6th, I just, you know, think about those calls that these number one dispatchers might have been receiving, you know, from the people working in and around the U.S. Capitol. That is real trauma, you know, that they are forced to endure, you know, for eight, 10, 12 hours a day, every single day that they're working, their human beings, after their shift is done, you know, their mental health needs to be taken under consideration. They cannot just be expected to go home and be that normal mom, the normal dad, take care of their children and just go about their day to day after hearing, you know, very traumatic incidents. There has to be You know, closure for these people in 17 and a half years spent working doing this job really gives me the very personal you know, experience, I think, that I need in order to try to convince my colleagues. And even at that, you know, politics is still politics. We have firefighters that are opposing this bill because, you know, their PSAPs are typically staffed with light-duty positions. We want to make it clear to them that the 911 saves at reclassifying these employees is not going to take away from those positions. We have to work together with them to remove that opposition so that we can get a hearing, so that we can continue to prioritize the needs of these professionals.",
