    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75272.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Right, and I think it speaks volumes to the lack of attention that we have paid to this profession, the lack of training to deal with the day-to-day stress of the job. I think about the two shootings that we had in California, one in Monterey Park, where it's very close to the LA, you know, area in Los Angeles County, where I know that these professionals are most likely post certified. You know, they probably have had a lot of training and they probably do have continuous training. And then I think about, you know, the other dispatchers that answer those calls, you know, in Northern California. And I think about this little tiny, you know, beach community with very, very, very little crime. And I think about what kind of training that those dispatchers have to deal with such a huge incident. And we know that it's not apples to apples, that we know that the training that they get, the professional training that they receive, either while they were just hired or if they have 10 years on the job. It's not the same because it's all based on the budget of those communities.",
